  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1018 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nRadiational cooling conditions have been disrupted across much of\nthe Mid-South due to a pesky post-frontal stratus deck. Mid-level\nwater vapor imagery shows a large, very dry area right along the\nTennessee/Mississippi state line where this cloud deck is slowly\nbeing eroded. Areas across southwest Arkansas and north\nMississippi will likely trend cooler than its northern neighbors\ndue to the cloud coverage.\n\nIt will be quite a few unseasonably cool days and even cooler\nmornings through much of the forecast period. While cloud\ncoverage will likely disrupt the first freeze across West\nTennessee, Sunday's probabilities remain between 40-60% for our\nnorthern zones and 25% or less for southern.\n\nHigh pressure will dominate through most of the forecast period\nleaving dry and cool conditions in for at least the first half of\nthe week. Projected highs are generally in the upper 60s and\nlower 70s, but given a dry air mass and known NBM biases,\ntemperatures may trend upwards into the low to mid 70s. Long\nrange guidance struggles with the evolution of a trough over Baja\nCalifornia and projected shortwaves to cross the Mid-South.\nCurrent probabilities from the NBM for receiving a trace of rain\nare negligible until Wednesday and Thursday. Enjoy the fall\nweather!\n\nDNM",
